syntax:
The basic syntax for exception handling is:
try {
    // Code that might throw an exception
} catch (const Type& e) {
    // Handle the exception
} catch (...) {
    // Catch-all for unexpected exceptions
}
Practical usage notes: Use 'throw' to raise exceptions, prefer standard exceptions like std::runtime_error, and always include necessary headers like <exception>. In real-world scenarios, nest try-catch blocks for modular error handling and combine with smart pointers to ensure resource safety.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>  // For std::runtime_error
int main() {
    try {
        // Simulate a real-world operation, e.g., file access or network call
        int value = 10;
        if (value == 10) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Value is invalid in this context");
        }
        // Rest of the code
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        // Handle the exception gracefully
        std::cout << "Exception caught: " << e.what() << std::endl;
        // Log the error or perform recovery actions
    }
    return 0;
}
